{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars answer categorical magnitude questions through length from a shared\nbaseline. Sort them when rank is part of the question, keep a semantic order\nwhen sequence matters, and use a lighter comparison mark when a filled bar\nwould overstate the data."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which category has the largest or smallest value?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorted bars"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which categories have notable endpoints without emphasizing area?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lollipops"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How far apart are two values for each category?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dumbbells"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do signed contributions bridge from a start to a total?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A waterfall"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do subgroups contribute to each category?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped or stacked bars in "},{"type":"link","href":"./stacked-and-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacked and Composed Charts"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use horizontal bars when labels are long or when the rank itself should read\ntop to bottom. "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layout, Axes, and Coordinates"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nshows how categorical orientation and automatic guide margins fit together."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rank-categories-with-bars","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rank categories with bars"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting the scale domain makes the intended ranking explicit. Sorting only the\ninput rows is insufficient when several layers or prepared datasets share the\nsame categorical axis."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"sorted-bars-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"sorted-bars","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\n\\nconst rows = [\\n  { category: 'Search', value: 84 },\\n  { category: 'Direct', value: 63 },\\n  { category: 'Referral', value: 47 },\\n  { category: 'Social', value: 31 },\\n]\\n\\nconst ranked = [...rows].sort((a, b) => b.value - a.value)\\n\\nconst chart = defineChart({\\n  marks: [barY(ranked, { x: 'category', y: 'value', inset: 2 })],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: () =>\\n        scaleBand<string>()\\n          .domain(ranked.map((row) => row.category))\\n          .padding(0.16),\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      nice: true,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Weekly signups' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\\n\\nexport default chart\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { category: 'Search', value: 84 },\n  { category: 'Direct', value: 63 },\n  { category: 'Referral', value: 47 },\n  { category: 'Social', value: 31 },\n]\n\nconst ranked = [...rows].sort((a, b) => b.value - a.value)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(ranked, { x: 'category', y: 'value', inset: 2 })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () =>\n        scaleBand<string>()\n          .domain(ranked.map((row) => row.category))\n          .padding(0.16),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Weekly signups' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart","lang":"ts","meta":"group=sorted-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-vertical-sorted/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the larger catalog case"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nto inspect responsive label rotation and data updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar charts normally include zero on the quantitative domain. Truncating that\nbaseline turns small differences into large apparent changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reduce-visual-weight-with-lollipops","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reduce visual weight with lollipops"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A lollipop keeps the common baseline and precise endpoint while replacing the\nfilled rectangle with a thin link. It is useful for many categories or when\nthe endpoint matters more than area."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"16-lollipop","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compose the stem and endpoint as separate marks. The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefines the link channels; "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dot and Hexagon Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefines the endpoint layer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-two-values-per-category","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare two values per category"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dumbbells emphasize the distance and direction between two endpoints without\nimplying the combined area of grouped bars."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"17-dumbbell","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Label the endpoint semantics in a legend or surrounding text. If chronological\norder between two periods is the message, a slopegraph may be more direct; if\nabsolute magnitudes must remain independently comparable, use grouped bars."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"explain-a-bridge-to-a-total","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain a bridge to a total"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A waterfall requires cumulative preparation. Each contribution becomes an\nexplicit lower and upper interval; the renderer should not guess whether a row\nis a delta, subtotal, or total."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"29-waterfall","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the cumulative calculation in application data preparation and pass the\nprepared interval channels to a ranged bar or rectangle. The ownership boundary\nis described in "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and the geometry\ncontracts are in "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar and Rect Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve a semantic category order unless rank is the question."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Include zero for ordinary bar magnitudes; use explicit interval endpoints for\nfloating and waterfall bars."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid encoding the same distinction only by color. Labels, position, and\nshape can carry the essential comparison."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify long labels and rotated ticks with\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve unique category values when bars reorder or animate; supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":"\nonly when the category does not identify a row. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic Data and Animation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"rank-categories-with-bars","text":"Rank categories with bars","level":2},{"id":"reduce-visual-weight-with-lollipops","text":"Reduce visual weight with lollipops","level":2},{"id":"compare-two-values-per-category","text":"Compare two values per category","level":2},{"id":"explain-a-bridge-to-a-total","text":"Explain a bridge to a total","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nBars answer categorical magnitude questions through length from a shared\nbaseline. Sort them when rank is part of the question, keep a semantic order\nwhen sequence matters, and use a lighter comparison mark when a filled bar\nwould overstate the data.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                                   | Start with                                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Which category has the largest or smallest value?                 | Sorted bars                                                                            |\n| Which categories have notable endpoints without emphasizing area? | Lollipops                                                                              |\n| How far apart are two values for each category?                   | Dumbbells                                                                              |\n| How do signed contributions bridge from a start to a total?       | A waterfall                                                                            |\n| How do subgroups contribute to each category?                     | Grouped or stacked bars in [Stacked and Composed Charts](./stacked-and-composition.md) |\n\nUse horizontal bars when labels are long or when the rank itself should read\ntop to bottom. [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md)\nshows how categorical orientation and automatic guide margins fit together.\n\n## Rank categories with bars\n\nSorting the scale domain makes the intended ranking explicit. Sorting only the\ninput rows is insufficient when several layers or prepared datasets share the\nsame categorical axis.\n\n```ts group=sorted-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { category: 'Search', value: 84 },\n  { category: 'Direct', value: 63 },\n  { category: 'Referral', value: 47 },\n  { category: 'Social', value: 31 },\n]\n\nconst ranked = [...rows].sort((a, b) => b.value - a.value)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(ranked, { x: 'category', y: 'value', inset: 2 })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () =>\n        scaleBand<string>()\n          .domain(ranked.map((row) => row.category))\n          .padding(0.16),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Weekly signups' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n[Open the larger catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-vertical-sorted/)\nto inspect responsive label rotation and data updates.\n\nBar charts normally include zero on the quantitative domain. Truncating that\nbaseline turns small differences into large apparent changes.\n\n## Reduce visual weight with lollipops\n\nA lollipop keeps the common baseline and precise endpoint while replacing the\nfilled rectangle with a thin link. It is useful for many categories or when\nthe endpoint matters more than area.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=16-lollipop height=480 -->\n\nCompose the stem and endpoint as separate marks. The\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\ndefines the link channels; [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md)\ndefines the endpoint layer.\n\n## Compare two values per category\n\nDumbbells emphasize the distance and direction between two endpoints without\nimplying the combined area of grouped bars.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=17-dumbbell height=480 -->\n\nLabel the endpoint semantics in a legend or surrounding text. If chronological\norder between two periods is the message, a slopegraph may be more direct; if\nabsolute magnitudes must remain independently comparable, use grouped bars.\n\n## Explain a bridge to a total\n\nA waterfall requires cumulative preparation. Each contribution becomes an\nexplicit lower and upper interval; the renderer should not guess whether a row\nis a delta, subtotal, or total.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=29-waterfall height=480 -->\n\nKeep the cumulative calculation in application data preparation and pass the\nprepared interval channels to a ranged bar or rectangle. The ownership boundary\nis described in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md), and the geometry\ncontracts are in [Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md).\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Preserve a semantic category order unless rank is the question.\n- Include zero for ordinary bar magnitudes; use explicit interval endpoints for\n  floating and waterfall bars.\n- Avoid encoding the same distinction only by color. Labels, position, and\n  shape can carry the essential comparison.\n- Verify long labels and rotated ticks with\n  [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Preserve unique category values when bars reorder or animate; supply `key`\n  only when the category does not identify a row. See\n  [Dynamic Data and Animation](../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Bars and Rankings\ndescription: Choose bar, lollipop, dumbbell, and waterfall compositions for categorical comparison and change.\n---\n\nBars answer categorical magnitude questions through length from a shared\nbaseline. Sort them when rank is part of the question, keep a semantic order\nwhen sequence matters, and use a lighter comparison mark when a filled bar\nwould overstate the data.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                                   | Start with                                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Which category has the largest or smallest value?                 | Sorted bars                                                                            |\n| Which categories have notable endpoints without emphasizing area? | Lollipops                                                                              |\n| How far apart are two values for each category?                   | Dumbbells                                                                              |\n| How do signed contributions bridge from a start to a total?       | A waterfall                                                                            |\n| How do subgroups contribute to each category?                     | Grouped or stacked bars in [Stacked and Composed Charts](./stacked-and-composition.md) |\n\nUse horizontal bars when labels are long or when the rank itself should read\ntop to bottom. [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md)\nshows how categorical orientation and automatic guide margins fit together.\n\n## Rank categories with bars\n\nSorting the scale domain makes the intended ranking explicit. Sorting only the\ninput rows is insufficient when several layers or prepared datasets share the\nsame categorical axis.\n\n```ts group=sorted-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { category: 'Search', value: 84 },\n  { category: 'Direct', value: 63 },\n  { category: 'Referral', value: 47 },\n  { category: 'Social', value: 31 },\n]\n\nconst ranked = [...rows].sort((a, b) => b.value - a.value)\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(ranked, { x: 'category', y: 'value', inset: 2 })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () =>\n        scaleBand<string>()\n          .domain(ranked.map((row) => row.category))\n          .padding(0.16),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Weekly signups' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n[Open the larger catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-vertical-sorted/)\nto inspect responsive label rotation and data updates.\n\nBar charts normally include zero on the quantitative domain. Truncating that\nbaseline turns small differences into large apparent changes.\n\n## Reduce visual weight with lollipops\n\nA lollipop keeps the common baseline and precise endpoint while replacing the\nfilled rectangle with a thin link. It is useful for many categories or when\nthe endpoint matters more than area.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=16-lollipop height=480 -->\n\nCompose the stem and endpoint as separate marks. The\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\ndefines the link channels; [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md)\ndefines the endpoint layer.\n\n## Compare two values per category\n\nDumbbells emphasize the distance and direction between two endpoints without\nimplying the combined area of grouped bars.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=17-dumbbell height=480 -->\n\nLabel the endpoint semantics in a legend or surrounding text. If chronological\norder between two periods is the message, a slopegraph may be more direct; if\nabsolute magnitudes must remain independently comparable, use grouped bars.\n\n## Explain a bridge to a total\n\nA waterfall requires cumulative preparation. Each contribution becomes an\nexplicit lower and upper interval; the renderer should not guess whether a row\nis a delta, subtotal, or total.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=29-waterfall height=480 -->\n\nKeep the cumulative calculation in application data preparation and pass the\nprepared interval channels to a ranged bar or rectangle. The ownership boundary\nis described in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md), and the geometry\ncontracts are in [Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md).\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Preserve a semantic category order unless rank is the question.\n- Include zero for ordinary bar magnitudes; use explicit interval endpoints for\n  floating and waterfall bars.\n- Avoid encoding the same distinction only by color. Labels, position, and\n  shape can carry the essential comparison.\n- Verify long labels and rotated ticks with\n  [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Preserve unique category values when bars reorder or animate; supply `key`\n  only when the category does not identify a row. See\n  [Dynamic Data and Animation](../guides/dynamic-data-and-animation.md).\n"}
